  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday morning through next Monday)\nIssued at 255 PM EDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nA mid-/upper-level ridge and associated high pressure will be in \nplace across much of the eastern CONUS on Thursday. The result \nwill be another day of high temperatures in the upper 80s to lower\n90s, which may match or break records.\n\nA cold front -- driven by a stout mid-/upper-level trough \nshifting across the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic -- will push \nthrough north and central Georgia between Friday afternoon and \nSaturday afternoon, bringing a noticeable drop in temperatures. \nLow temps on Saturday morning are forecast to be some 10-15 \ndegrees cooler than Friday morning, with similar differences \nbetween Saturday afternoon high temps and Friday afternoon high \ntemps. Temperatures post-FROPA will be near-normal for mid-\nOctober. Notably, lows in the mountains on Saturday, Sunday, and \nMonday mornings are expected to be in the upper 30s to lower 40s, \nwith mid-40s to upper 40s as far south as LaGrange to Macon to \nSwainsboro.\n\nAccompanying the cold front will be gusty conditions, with \nsustained winds of 15-20 mph and gusts of 25-35 mph on the table. \nThe cooler conditions should hinder any fire weather concerns, \neven amid the dry, gusty weather. The drying, stabilizing effects \nof high pressure in the days leading up to the FROPA will limit \nmoisture return ahead of the aforementioned trough, so rain \nchances remain essentially zero through the long-term period.",
